@begin(section)

@title(Architecture)

This page explains how zick is put together: the layout of the project,
the shape of the store, the lifecycle of a package, and how zick pairs
with the dependency resolver.

@begin(section)
@title(Project Layout)

zick is a Common Lisp port of @link[uri="https://github.com/djhaskin987/zic"](zic),
and its source files mirror zic's Clojure modules one for one:

@begin(list)
@item(@c(src/main.lisp) — the CLI entry point.  Uses
@link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/cliff"](CLIFF) for argument
parsing, config files, environment variables, and subcommand dispatch;
each subcommand function calls into the package layer and returns a
hash table CLIFF prints as an NRDL document.)
@item(@c(src/session.lisp) — database sessions and the zick lock file.
The "connection" is the NRDL store slurped from disk; the lock is a
plain lock file whose very existence is the lock.)
@item(@c(src/db.lisp) — the store: FSet persistent records serialized
as a single NRDL document, plus the queries and mutations over them.)
@item(@c(src/package.lisp) — package install, removal, upgrade, and
verification on top of the session and db layers.)
@item(@c(src/fs.lisp) — filesystem and archive helpers: zip reading,
SHA-256 and CRC-32 checksums, downloads, unpacking, and the marking
file search.)
@end(list)

The CLI itself is data-driven: @c(main) hands CLIFF a table of
subcommand names to functions, a default function, defaults for the
options, and a @c(setup) function that derives the database connection
string, project root, staging path, and lock path from the marking
file.  CLIFF handles the rest — config files, environment variables,
aliases, output.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Finding the Project)

A zick project is marked by a directory named @c(.zick-db) somewhere at
or above the start directory (which defaults to the current working
directory, or is given with @c(-d)).  zick walks up the directory tree
looking for it; the first match names the project root.

For compatibility, a legacy @c(.zic-db) directory is accepted with a
warning printed to standard error.

From the marking directory, the setup function derives everything else:

@begin(list)
@item(@c(db-connection-string) — the @c(.zick-db) directory itself, the
"connection" to the store)
@item(@c(root-path) — the parent of the marking directory, where
package files live)
@item(@c(staging-path) — a @c(.staging) directory under the root, where
archives are downloaded before unpacking)
@item(@c(lock-path) — a @c(.zick.lock) file under the root)
@end(list)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(The Store)

The store is a single NRDL document, @c(packages.nrdl), inside the
@c(.zick-db) directory.  It holds two maps:

@begin(list)
@item(@c(packages) — package name to package record.  A package record
has a name, version, location (the archive URL), metadata (an FSet map,
honored for its @c(zick) key), whether it is a source package, and the
set of packages it depends on.)
@item(@c(files) — path to file record.  A file record has a path, size,
class (@c(normal-file), @c(config-file), or @c(ghost-file)), an SHA-256
checksum, and the name of the package that owns it.)
@end(list)

Values live in FSet land throughout — the store, its maps, sets, and
seqs are persistent FSet structures.  Conversion happens only at the
I/O boundary: reading parses the NRDL document back into FSet
structures (NRDL emits sets, seqs, and maps natively), and writing
serializes the records directly.

The store is read and written whole: a session slurps the document,
the package layer transforms it, and the session saves whatever store
its thunk returns.  Saves are atomic — a temp file is written and
renamed over the target.  @c(zick init) creates the store up front;
commands run without @c(init) create it on demand the first time they
open it.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Sessions and Locking)

Every mutation runs inside a zick session (@c(with-zick-session)),
which takes the lock and then opens the database, so that concurrent
zick processes serialize on the lock before either reads the store.

The lock is a lock file:acquiring it is creating @c(.zick.lock) atomically (with exclusive-create semantics), and releasing it is
deleting the file.  If another zick process holds the lock, zick
signals a descriptive error.  Because the lock is the file's
existence, a process killed with @c(SIGKILL) leaves a stale lock file
behind; remove it manually to recover.

zick only needs to coordinate with other zick processes, so this
advisory lock file is all that is required — no OS-level file lock.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(The Package Lifecycle)

@begin(section)
@title(Install)

@c(zick add) with downloading enabled (the default) proceeds as
follows:

@begin(enum)
@item(Download.  The archive at the package location is fetched into
the @c(.staging) directory.  Per-host authorizations (basic, header,
or bearer-token) and TLS verification are honored as configured.  The
archive is opened and its CRC-32s are checked; a zip with CRC
violations is refused.)
@item(Check conflicts.  Every non-directory entry must not already be
owned by another package; if it is, install signals an error.)
@item(Decide configuration fate.  Files listed under the metadata's
@c(zick) @c(config-files) key are compared against what is installed
and what is on disk.  A config file not yet on disk is installed; one
already on disk with no installed version to compare against is put
aside (written as @c(path.PKG.VER.new)); a config unchanged by the
upgrade keeps its current contents.)
@item(Unpack.  The archive's entries are written into the project
root.  Files put aside land under unique paths, and excluded files are
not written.)
@item(Record.  The package and every unpacked file (path, size, class,
checksum, owner) are added to the store, along with the package's
dependency edges.)
@end(enum)

With @c(-W) (downloading disabled), the package is recorded from its
options alone: no network, no unpacking, no files.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Upgrade)

Re-adding a package that is already installed upgrades it: the old
files are removed, configuration files are handled by the
configuration-fate logic above (with the old config's checksum
preserved so unchanged configs survive), and the store is updated in
place.  Replacing a package with a version that compares equal counts
as an in-place replacement, and re-adding at a lower version counts as
a downgrade; both are errors unless the corresponding option
(@c(-i)/@c(--enable-allow-inplace), @c(-g)/@c(--enable-allow-downgrades))
is enabled.  Version comparison is Debian-style.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Verify)

@c(zick verify) checks the files on disk against the store.  Normal
files must exist, have the recorded size, and hash to the recorded
SHA-256.  Configuration files must exist.  Ghost files must not be
present.  Each failing file is grouped by the kind of failure:
@c(file-missing), @c(size-discrepancy), @c(checksum-discrepancy),
@c(config-file-missing), @c(path-not-file), or
@c(path-not-directory).

@c(verify) is how zick earns its keep in a checkout or a deployment:
a @c(git clean), a partial restore, or a bad deploy shows up as a
non-zero exit with an exact list of what is wrong.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Remove)

@c(zick remove) deletes the files a package owns (backing up its
configuration files to @c(path.PKG.VER.backup)) and removes its record
and dependency edges.  Removing a package others depend on is an error
unless @c(-c)/@c(--enable-cascade) is given, in which case the
dependent packages are removed too, sinks first (a package nothing
depends on is removed before the packages that depend on it).  With
@c(-r)/@c(--enable-dry-run), the removal is computed and reported
without touching the tree or the store.

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Orphans)

The orphaned packages are the source nodes of the dependency graph:
the installed packages that nothing depends on.  @c(zick orphans)
reports them, and they are what a cascade removal starts from.

@end(section)

@end(section)

@begin(section)
@title(Pairing with the Resolver)

zick and @link[uri="https://github.com/djha-skin/dsolv"](dsolv) are
independent tools with complementary architectures:

@begin(list)
@item(dsolv is a generic dependency resolver: it reads a repository of
cards describing versions and requirements, and returns the exact set
of versions a build needs — the URLs of the files to download.)
@item(zick is a package installer: it downloads those URLs, unpacks
them into the project tree, tracks the files it owns, and verifies
them on disk.)
@end(list)

In a build the two fit together like this: dsolv resolves which
versions are needed and hands zick the URLs; zick fetches those exact
archives, installs them, and proves they are intact.  zick has no
opinion about version selection — it takes the versions it is given —
and dsolv has no opinion about file layout — it returns locations.
That division keeps each tool small and lets them be used separately.

@end(section)

@end(section)
